TWO GOVERNANCE-FILE FIXES, both approved by the owner in writing (approval
recorded verbatim on the tracker items).

1. `merge-pr` printed a codeberg.org link on a GitHub remote.

`origin` is git@github.com:iterabloom/hypergumbo.git and the API layer already
routed correctly — detect_forge_backend() sets FORGE_BACKEND and
detect_api_base() sends calls to api.github.com. Only the DISPLAY strings were
never told. This was one fact in two homes with the wrong home winning:
auto-pr carried a correct private `_autopr_pr_web_url`, while merge-pr
hardcoded `https://codeberg.org/$REPO_SLUG/pulls/$n` in two places. GitHub's
web path is /pull/N SINGULAR, so the emitted links were wrong in host AND path.

pr_web_url() moves into scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh, which both scripts already
source; the private copy is deleted and all five call sites repointed. The
non-GitHub branch derives its host from API_BASE instead of naming
codeberg.org — previously a self-hosted Forgejo user got a link to somebody
else's server. Verified live: github -> /pull/552, codeberg -> /pulls/552,
git.example.org -> /pulls/552.

Nothing checks a printed link, which is why this survived: the API worked and
CI was green. Now pinned by
test_no_script_hardcodes_a_forge_host_in_a_pr_link.

Left alone deliberately: auto-pr's comment examples at lines 509/512 still
show codeberg URLs. They are illustrative prose, not emitted output, and
editing them widens a governance diff for no behavioural gain.

2. The WI-bunag closure guard's rejection now warns against quoting itself.

The guard refuses a merge when a bare Closes|Fixes|Resolves names a still-open
item. Its two documented remedies are to close the item or use the
Closes-with-evidence form. Neither covers the case where the reference was
simply wrong wording, so the reader rewrites the message — and the obvious
rewrite explains what happened, quoting the phrase that caused it. The scan is
a regex over the whole message with no notion of quotation or disapproval, so
that rewrite re-trips the same guard verbatim.

The rejection now says to describe the change rather than the rejection, and
ends with a pre-push check:

    git log -1 --format=%B | grep -inE '(closes|fixes|resolves)[[:space:]:]+(WI|INV|META)-'

That check is the load-bearing half. The guard fires at MERGE time, after CI
has already run, so a message defect costs a full cycle every time; the grep
runs before the push and costs nothing. Pinned by
test_rejection_tells_you_not_to_narrate_it_in_the_rewrite.

The merge-pr stub in tests/test_merge_pr_close.py gains pr_web_url —
TestStubMirrorsTheRealLibrary caught the omission, which would otherwise have
surfaced as a bash 127 inside an unrelated-looking assertion.

